  "text": "Register an artist. Build their identity once \u2014 how they look on screen, likeness consent on record. Attach a song; it's measured once: tempo, drop, hook windows \u2014 and no tempo without a method behind it.\n\nThen generate \u2014 whatever the release needs. A performance clip. A press still. The artist announcing a show, straight to camera. Or a backdrop built to be copied, so a fan who sees one knows instantly what their own version looks like.\n\nEvery one of those is a format, and a format is a row a label can edit \u2014 not a shape we welded in.\n\nBuild it once, and the second video is cheap. Cost per attempt is the only lever left.\n\nHere's what runs it. AWS handles the logic. Backblaze B2 holds the bytes \u2014 and the proof.\n\nThat split is why this system has no database. A label stores documents, not rows \u2014 an artist, its measurements, a finished kit \u2014 so they live in a B2 bucket. Eleven services became five. Idle cost: under a dollar a month.\n\nB2 does four jobs.\n\nIt's the upload path \u2014 masters go browser straight to bucket, never touching our server.\n\nIt's the catalog. Every run writes under tenant, date, run ID, with the index landing as Parquet in the same bucket, queryable by Athena. No warehouse.\n\nIt's the receipt. Every asset lands beside a manifest naming the provider, model, and prompt behind every step \u2014 verified before a kit ships. Unverified fails.\n\nAnd it's the disclosure. Download an asset and that manifest is embedded inside the file, while the stored object stays byte-identical, so the hash still means something. Drop that file into our verify endpoint and it says exactly how it was made. Nothing is looked up. The proof is in the bytes.\n\nYou cannot buy a hit. You can only lower the cost of trying \u2014 and be honest about what came out.\n\nBring us one artist and one song.",
